From 280c284ebc9f0800bc07ee4376de3ae2647668d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 11:16:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] rtemstoolkit: Never treat an undefined symbol as a definition

file::get_symbols() routed a symbol to the unresolved set only when it was
STT_NOTYPE, STB_GLOBAL and SHN_UNDEF.  Anything else undefined fell through
to the second test, which looks only at the type and the binding, so a weak
undefined symbol - or an undefined global that carries a type - was
collected as a definition and handed to the tools as one.

GNU ld drops unresolved weak undefined symbols from the symbol table of a
fully linked image, so this stayed hidden.  lld keeps them.  Linking an
RTEMS image with lld therefore left entries such as
_ITM_registerTMCloneTable and _ZGTtnaj from libstdc++ in .symtab, rtems-syms
harvested them into the base image global symbol table, and every libdl
test failed to link because the generated table referenced symbols nothing
defines.

Test only the binding and the section index, so an undefined external is
unresolved whatever its type.  The binding is still checked because the ELF
null symbol is local and undefined and must not be picked up: without that
rtems-ld reports "symbol not found: " for the empty name.

Output for a GNU ld linked image is unchanged, byte for byte.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 rtemstoolkit/rld-elf.cpp | 20 +++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/rtemstoolkit/rld-elf.cpp b/rtemstoolkit/rld-elf.cpp
index b8b5688..2e3a237 100644
--- a/rtemstoolkit/rld-elf.cpp
+++ b/rtemstoolkit/rld-elf.cpp
@@ -860,16 +860,22 @@ namespace rld
         int sbind = sym.binding ();
 
         /*
-         * If wanting unresolved symbols and the type is no-type and the
-         * section is undefined, or, the type is no-type or object or function
-         * and the bind is local and we want local symbols, or the bind is weak
-         * and we want weak symbols, or the bind is global and we want global
-         * symbols then add the filtered symbols container.
+         * An external symbol with an undefined section index defines nothing,
+         * whatever its type, so it is only ever an unresolved symbol.  This
+         * covers a weak undefined symbol too: a linker may keep one in the
+         * symbol table of a fully linked image (lld does, GNU ld does not),
+         * and taking it for a definition makes the tools emit a reference to a
+         * symbol nothing defines.  The binding is checked so that the ELF null
+         * symbol, which is local and undefined, is not mistaken for one.
+         *
+         * Otherwise, if the type is no-type or object or function and the bind
+         * is local and we want local symbols, or the bind is weak and we want
+         * weak symbols, or the bind is global and we want global symbols then
+         * add to the filtered symbols container.
          */
         bool add = false;
 
-        if ((stype == STT_NOTYPE) &&
-            (sbind == STB_GLOBAL) &&
+        if (((sbind == STB_GLOBAL) || (sbind == STB_WEAK)) &&
             (sym.section_index () == SHN_UNDEF))
         {
           if (unresolved)
